<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
     AresLauncher - the widget resize handle (S3): an L tracing the tile's bottom-end corner.

     Replaces a chevron on a filled circle. That read as a BUTTON, which is the wrong promise now
     that the affordance is dragged rather than tapped: "right now it looks like a buttom. Maybe a
     corner hook would be better? ... like a curved L traciong the boarder of the corner of the
     square".

     Arms deliberately SHORT. The first cut ran them 11 units into a 26-unit box and read as a
     bracket framing the corner rather than a mark sitting on it - "Id like to make it a tad smaller
     or shorter, and lets move it to the boarder". They are now 3 units of straight arm either side of the elbow, in a 22-unit box, and the
     view's margin was dropped so the whole thing sits on the tile's edge instead of inside it.

     The elbow is a quarter-circle rather than a mitre so it follows the same curve the tile's own
     corner has (ares_edit_cell_outline_radius), which is what makes it read as part of the edge.
     Round caps for the same reason - a square-cut arm ends in a hard tick that reads as a separate
     mark.

     Stroke, no fill, no backdrop: a filled shape here would be the button we are getting away from.
     In edit mode this always sits on the frost box, which is what it reads against.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="22dp"
    android:height="22dp"
    android:viewportWidth="22"
    android:viewportHeight="22"
    android:tint="?android:attr/textColorPrimary">

    <path
        android:pathData="M10,19 H13 A6,6 0 0 0 19,13 V10"
        android:strokeColor="@android:color/white"
        android:strokeWidth="2.25"
        android:strokeLineCap="round"
        android:strokeLineJoin="round" />
</vector>
